"In this surah, Allah the Exalted addresses the Messenger of Allah, saying: \"Indeed, We have granted you al-Kawthar.\" In Arabic, Kawthar is derived from the root kathrah, meaning abundance, and denotes something that is exceedingly abundant. In the lexicon, it signifies the majority of something, an extraordinarily benevolent man, a most generous giver, and a specific river in Paradise. Here, the fact that what is given is mentioned by Allah with immense majesty demonstrates that it indeed signifies an eternal and infinite abundance. The first obvious meaning understood is 'khayr kathir', that is, abundant good.\n\nRegarding what Kawthar is, the commentators have enumerated up to twenty-six opinions. One of the most famous is that Kawthar is a river in Paradise. This meaning is established through sound hadiths. In the hadiths, it is reported that this river is whiter than milk, sweeter than honey, its width is as vast as the distance between the east and the west, and the Ummah will drink from it on the Day of Resurrection. Furthermore, a basin belonging to our Prophet is also mentioned, which some say is located on the Gathering Plain. However, belief in these basins is not obligatory; yet, because the hadiths concerning our Prophet's basin are well-known, belief in it has been deemed obligatory by the Ahl al-Sunnah.\n\nAccording to the second opinion, Kawthar is the honor of prophethood. Prophethood is an abundant good that encompasses happiness in this world and the Hereafter. The third opinion is the scholars of the Ummah. As the inheritors of the prophets, the scholars draw grace from the source of revelation and teach good to the Ummah. The fourth opinion is the great number of the Ummah. Allah has granted the Messenger of Allah so many noble Companions and such an abundant Ummah that more than half of the people of Paradise will be from his Ummah. The fifth opinion is the abundance of our Prophet's descendants. This surah was revealed as an answer to his enemies who called him 'abtar' (one cut off from lineage) when his son died. Allah gave the glad tidings that his lineage would not be severed, but rather that He would grant him an abundant progeny.\n\nThe majority of commentators have accepted Kawthar as 'abundant good' because this is its broadest lexical meaning. The other meanings are also included within this concept of 'abundant good'. The famous view narrated from Ibn Abbas is likewise that Kawthar is the good that Allah bestowed upon His Messenger. The description of the river in the hadiths can be understood as the most prominent example of this good. For this reason, while accepting the hadiths about the river, commentators have preferred the meaning of 'abundant good' by considering all the possibilities of the wording.\n\nThat Kawthar is a river in Paradise is supported especially by the hadiths of the Mi'raj. This river was shown to our Prophet during the Mi'raj, and great manifestations occurred near the Sidrat al-Muntaha. This meaning points to eternal and sacred objectives of good. However, interpreting it as 'abundant good' in a way that everyone can understand is more comprehensive and beneficial. Allah's expression of the verb 'We have given' in the past tense shows that this bounty was an accomplished fact realized while in this world. All future goods are also evaluated within this giving. Kawthar is an uninterrupted and infinite gift encompassing the goods of both this world and the Hereafter.\n\nKawthar is a bounty specific to the Prophet and the source of the rivers of Paradise. In this surah, the statement 'We have given you al-Kawthar' in the past tense carries the meaning of an announcement from pre-eternity, an establishment in the present, and a commitment for the future. This gives the glad tidings that all goods in this world and the Hereafter—blessings such as knowledge, wisdom, prophethood, the Quran, and sublime character—have been bestowed upon the Prophet and his Ummah. This favor is continuous, and the Prophet's family, his Companions, and the Followers (tabi'un) will also partake in this grace.\n\nAs gratitude for this blessing of Kawthar, the Prophet was commanded to perform prayer. The letter 'fa' indicates that this command is connected to the preceding favor. Prayer is the foremost of acts of worship, encompassing every form of gratitude. The qualification 'pray for your Lord' emphasizes that the prayer must be performed purely for Allah's pleasure, with sincerity. This is the essence of prayer and requires staying away from ostentation.\n\nAlong with the command of prayer came the command of sacrificial slaughter. It carries the meaning of 'pray for your Lord, and sacrifice for your Lord,' and it is a condition that both acts of worship be performed solely for Allah. The sacrifice is an act of worship requiring financial sacrifice and was made obligatory specifically for the Prophet. This command also encompasses sacrificing after the prayer and may point to the Eid prayer.\n\nThere are differing views regarding which prayer the command refers to: the obligatory prayers, supererogatory prayers, the Eid prayer, or the mid-morning (duha) prayer. In addition to the five daily prayers, the tahajjud and duha prayers were also obligatory for the Prophet. Since the Eid prayer had not yet been made obligatory in Mecca, it can be considered that this command was directed toward the duha prayer. However, there are also those who state that the surah is Medinan.\n\nRegarding the command to sacrifice, it has been narrated that while it was obligatory for the Prophet, it was not made obligatory for the Ummah. In the Hanafi school, sacrifice is considered wajib. This command refers to the udhiyyah sacrifice slaughtered after the Eid prayer. The word 'nahr' means slaughtering, and the principle that this worship must be performed for Allah is a requirement of Islam's understanding of tawhid.\n\nThe word \"nahr\" in the second verse of Surah al-Kawthar has generally been interpreted as \"to sacrifice an animal.\" Elmalılı states that beyond the slaughter of camels, this word encompasses all cattle and small livestock, though it is more commonly used for camels. Furthermore, he emphasizes that the command in the verse entails performing prayer along with sacrificing an animal for the pleasure of Allah.\n\nHadiths concerning the sacrificial practices of our Noble Prophet are cited: his slaughtering of a ram, his slaughtering the sacrifice himself while saying \"Bismillahi wallahu akbar,\" and matters such as a camel sufficing as a sacrifice for seven people. Elmalılı notes that the most virtuous sacrifice is a ram, yet points out the camel's great size.\n\nAlthough some scholars have interpreted \"nahr\" as raising the hands to the chest in prayer or facing the qiblah, Elmalılı rejects this view. According to him, the clear meaning of the verse is sacrificial slaughter; contrary interpretations rely on reports with weak chains of narration and do not accord with the context of the verse.\n\nThe words \"shani'\" (the one who hates) and \"abtar\" (docked, cut off) mentioned in the third verse are explained. Elmalılı states that this verse signifies that those who harbor animosity toward the Prophet are the ones who are truly cut off, and that their lineages and good fortunes will be severed. The verse is an answer to those who called the Messenger of Allah 'abtar' due to the death of his sons.\n\nAt the end of the surah, gratitude and worship to Allah are emphasized with the command, \"Pray for your Lord and sacrifice.\" Elmalılı expresses that these verses are connected to similar commands in Surahs al-Duha and al-An'am, and that Kawthar is an uninterrupted good. He also dwells upon the wisdom behind the Prophet's lineage continuing through his daughter."